<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn al-Thumna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1062</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn al-Thumna was one of the rival Muslim lords who divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the 1050s, ruling Syracuse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>. Defeated by his rival Ibn al-Hawwas, he went to Count Roger in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> in 1061 and invited the Normans to intervene, helping them take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>. He was killed in 1062, but the Norman conquest he had invited went on until 1091.</p></div>
